---
title: Element 3: How Long will Data be Made Available
---
sort_key: 3
---
menu: /help/guides/nih
---
body:

#### Question

Will shared scientific data be made available for at least as long as required by applicable data repository policies and/or journal policies?

#### Guidance for Response

A response of "Yes" is expected. "As long as required" may have different meanings in different contexts. It generally refers to a retention period for the shared data, or, a commitment to make it available via the repository through which it was shared for a certain amount of time.

Retention periods to consider (and thus, how long to make the data available) include:

- Institutional data retention periods
- Journal data retention periods, as outlined in their publication requirements
- Repository policies for data retention

!!! info
    **Zenodo note:** Zenodo's features meet the primary [Desirable Characteristics for All Data Repositories](https://sharing.nih.gov/data-management-and-sharing-policy/sharing-scientific-data/selecting-a-data-repository#desirable-characteristics-for-all-data-repositories) outlined by the NIH. These include issuing a unique persistent identifier (DOI), provenance, and long-term sustainability. Zenodo retains items for the lifetime of the repository. Per Zenodo's [General Policies](https://about.zenodo.org/policies/) page, this is currently the lifetime of the host laboratory CERN, which currently has an experimental programme defined for the next 20 years at least.

---
